%I A306666 #18 Nov 11 2026 22:47:31
%S A306666 1,2,3,7,21
%N A306666 Positive integers x such that x*(7-5*x+x^2)*(6-4*x+x^2) is a square.
%C A306666 No other terms below 10^6.
%C A306666 No other terms below 10^10. - _Chai Wah Wu_, Nov 24 2019
%C A306666 The sequence is complete. Proof: let g = x^2-5x+7 and h = x^2-4x+6, both positive for all x. Since gcd(x,g) | 7, gcd(x,h) | 6 and gcd(g,h) | 3, every term has x = d1*a^2, g = d2*b^2, h = d3*c^2 with squarefree d1 | 42, d2 | 21, d3 | 6 and d1*d2*d3 a square. Of these 16 cases, congruences modulo 16, 9, 27, 49, 5, 25, 11, 13, 17, 19, 23 and 216 rule out all but (d1,d2,d3) = (1,3,3), (2,1,2), (3,1,3), (7,21,3), (21,7,3), (42,7,6). In each, Y^2 = d2*g(d1*a^2) is a quartic with a known rational point, and Magma's IntegralQuarticPoints (see Program) lists all its integral points: |a| = 1,2,5; 1; 1; 1; 0,1; 0, giving x = 1, 4, 25, 2, 3, 7, 0, 21, 0. Of these only 1, 2, 3, 7, 21 satisfy the original condition. - _Zachary Kleckner_, Nov 09 2026
%H A306666 dxdy forum, <a href="https://dxdy.ru/post1379764.html">x(7-5x+x^2)(6-4x+x^2)=y^2 in natural numbers</a> (in Russian).
%t A306666 Select[Range[1000], IntegerQ[Sqrt[#*(7 - 5*# + #^2)*(6 - 4*# + #^2)]] &] (* _Vaclav Kotesovec_, Mar 10 2019 *)
%o A306666 (PARI) isok(x)=issquare(x*(7-5*x+x^2)*(6-4*x+x^2));
%o A306666 (Python)
%o A306666 from sympy.ntheory.primetest import is_square
%o A306666 A306666_list = [n for n in range(1,10**3) if is_square(n*(n*(n*(n*(n - 9) + 33) - 58) + 42))] # _Chai Wah Wu_, Nov 24 2019
%o A306666 (Magma) // all integral points of the 6 descent quartics Y^2 = c4*a^4+c3*a^3+c2*a^2+c1*a+c0, each with a known point
%o A306666 for q in [ <[3,0,-15,0,21],[1,3]>, <[4,0,-10,0,7],[1,1]>, <[9,0,-15,0,7],[1,1]>, <[1029,0,-735,0,147],[1,21]>, <[3087,0,-735,0,49],[1,49]>, <[12348,0,-1470,0,49],[0,7]> ] do IntegralQuarticPoints(q[1], q[2]); end for;  // _Zachary Kleckner_, Nov 09 2026
